WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.477
AWS credentials are sensitive operational
authority.

2
00:00:04.557 --> 00:00:08.957
They may exist in shared credentials
files, AWS config files,

3
00:00:09.037 --> 00:00:13.352
SSO sessions, environment variables, CI/CD
secret stores,

4
00:00:13.432 --> 00:00:19.778
instance metadata roles, container
credential providers, or temporary session files.

5
00:00:19.858 --> 00:00:25.358
A script does not need to print a secret
for that secret to influence behavior.

6
00:00:25.438 --> 00:00:30.431
The presence of a credential is enough to
define what the script can do.

7
00:00:30.511 --> 00:00:36.011
Long-lived static credentials should be
minimized where better options are

8
00:00:36.091 --> 00:00:36.937
available.

9
00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:43.025
SSO, federation, MFA, role assumption,
short-lived sessions, workload identity,

10
00:00:43.105 --> 00:00:48.266
and metadata-based roles can reduce risk
when they fit the environment.

11
00:00:48.346 --> 00:00:51.392
They do not remove the need for
validation,

12
00:00:51.472 --> 00:00:57.057
but they can reduce the useful lifetime of
exposed credentials and make access

13
00:00:57.137 --> 00:00:58.406
easier to govern.

14
00:00:58.486 --> 00:01:04.663
Scripts should check whether required
credentials are available without displaying

15
00:01:04.743 --> 00:01:05.166
them.

16
00:01:05.246 --> 00:01:10.577
Avoid printing access keys, session
tokens, credential-derived values,

17
00:01:10.657 --> 00:01:16.580
full environments, debug traces, or
support bundles that include sensitive data.

18
00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:21.568
CI/CD masking is helpful, but it is not a
complete security boundary.

19
00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:27.063
Secrets can still appear through
transformations, files, command echoes,

20
00:01:27.143 --> 00:01:29.682
verbose tool output, or artifacts.

21
00:01:29.762 --> 00:01:36.108
Credential handling includes rotation,
revocation, ownership, and session duration.

22
00:01:36.188 --> 00:01:39.488
Teams should know how to expire a role
session,

23
00:01:39.568 --> 00:01:44.391
rotate a key if one still exists, disable
a compromised credential,

24
00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:47.517
and identify which jobs or scripts used
it.

25
00:01:47.597 --> 00:01:52.505
Secure AWS CLI automation treats
credentials as temporary, scoped,

26
00:01:52.585 --> 00:01:56.900
and reviewable rather than permanent
background plumbing.
